|> Section
    title=Simulating a biased coin using unbiased coin tosses

    Let us now consider the reverse problem of simulating
    a biased coin toss with bias $p$ when our source of
    randomness produces independent _unbiased_ bits
    $X_1,X_2,\ldots \sim \operatorname{Ber}(1/2)$. Of
    course, here we assume that the value $p$ of the
    desired bias is known, otherwise the question makes no
    sense. It turns out that in this case too there is a
    simple method, and moreover the method works extremely
    well even if $p$ is a very complicated number such as
    $1/\sqrt{2}$ or $\pi-3$.

    The method is based on the easy-to-prove observation
    that the random variable
    $U=\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{X_n}{2^n}$ has
    distribution $U[0,1]$. Then the indicator random
    variable $Z = 1_{\{U\le p\}}$ is a
    $\operatorname{Ber}(p)$ random variable. It only
    remains to note that $Z$ can be computed efficiently
    by uncovering the random bits $X_1,X_2,\ldots$ one at
    a time and stopping the computation as soon as it
    becomes apparent whether the event $\{U\le p\}$
    occurred or its complement $\{U>p\}$. The way this
    question is settled is described as follows.

    $\textbf{Simulation Method 2.}$ Let
    $p=(0.\alpha_1 \alpha_2 \alpha_3 \ldots )_2$ be the
    binary expansion of $p$, that is,
    $\alpha_n\in \{0,1\}$ and
    $p=\sum_{n=1}^\infty \alpha_n/2^{n}$.

    |> ol
        class=list
        data-list-style=decimal
        |> li
            Sample the random bits $X_1,X_2,\ldots$ one at a time
            until the first time $m\ge1$ such that
            $X_m \neq \alpha_m$.

        |> li
            If $(X_m,\alpha_m)=(0,1)$, output ``$1$''.

        |> li
            If $(X_m,\alpha_m)=(1,0)$, output ``$0$''.

    |> Exercise
        Prove that the output is $1$ if $U< p$; $0$ if $U>p$;
        and the algorithm never terminates in the event (which
        has probability 0) that $U=p$.

    |> Exercise
        Show that the number $N$ of bits which had to be
        sampled to obtain an answer satisfies
        $\mathbf{E} N = 2$. What is the distribution of $N$?